WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.327
Many teams inherit C++ code with raw arrays, pointer arithmetic, manual buffer management, and unclear ownership.

2
00:00:06.327 --> 00:00:09.886
The right response is not usually a full rewrite.

3
00:00:09.886 --> 00:00:15.026
Bounds safety improves through focused, incremental changes that reduce risk while preserving behavior.

4
00:00:15.026 --> 00:00:16.608
Start with priority paths.

5
00:00:16.608 --> 00:00:23.726
Externally reachable parsers, file importers, protocol handlers, serialization code, plugin boundaries, boundary-heavy loops, and code that mixes

6
00:00:23.726 --> 00:00:26.098
offsets and lengths deserve early attention.

7
00:00:26.098 --> 00:00:30.843
These paths are where unclear bounds assumptions can have the broadest impact.

8
00:00:30.843 --> 00:00:33.611
Add regression tests before behavior-sensitive changes.

9
00:00:33.611 --> 00:00:39.938
Legacy code often contains undocumented compatibility behavior, and tests give the team confidence to refactor safely.

10
00:00:39.938 --> 00:00:46.265
Boundary tests around empty input, exact sizes, truncated data, and near-limit ranges are especially useful.

11
00:00:46.265 --> 00:00:48.638
Wrap unsafe interfaces before replacing internals.

12
00:00:48.638 --> 00:00:55.755
A small adapter can validate inputs, preserve length with data, and provide a bounded view to newer code.

13
00:00:55.755 --> 00:01:03.269
That wrapper becomes a controlled boundary while the implementation is gradually moved toward containers, spans, and clearer range objects.

14
00:01:03.269 --> 00:01:06.827
Replace pointer arithmetic with clearer range logic where practical.

15
00:01:06.827 --> 00:01:15.922
Instead of moving pointers through a buffer without visible context, keep a view of remaining data or use helper functions that validate subranges.

16
00:01:15.922 --> 00:01:23.040
Document assumptions that cannot yet be removed and add follow-up work when the surrounding design is ready.
